WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.901 --> 00:00:02.067
-Hi, Mika.
Hi, Joe.

00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:03.234
-Hey.
-Welcome. Welcome back.

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.501
I haven't seen you in-studio
in a while.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:05.601
-It's been a long time.
-It's been a long time.

00:00:05.601 --> 00:00:06.667
-It's been a long time.

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.334
-Yeah, well, congrats
on everything.

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:10.334
Earlier today,
you launched a fourth --

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:12.100
the fourth hour
of "Morning Joe."

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.968
-Look what she brought out.
-Just in case.

00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:15.701
-Did you bring your phone?
-Yeah, I did.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:17.334
-That's one of my favorite --
-Do you want to take it away?

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:19.167
-No, I want you to get
a buzz first because that's

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:21.033
what I watch every --
I watch you every morning.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.701
And every now and then,
you get, like, a buzz

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:24.767
on your phone, and I can see
that it drives you crazy.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.300
And you're like --
-He gets so mad.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.801
-He's like, "Mika, can you just
put your phone somewhere?"

00:00:28.801 --> 00:00:30.367
And you're like,
"No, but I'm getting a link.

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.767
I'm giving it to the producer."
-My brothers, whoever.

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:35.934
-Yes, exactly.
People are just texting you...

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:37.567
-Exactly.
-...links to important things...

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.133
-Contacts.
-...and Joe is stopping you

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.534
from delivering the news.
-Exactly.

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:41.734
-Exactly.
-He's getting in the way.

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:42.734
-So I'm getting your back.
-Important things.

00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:44.434
-Thank you.
-Important things.

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:46.167
-Not even close.
-No, exactly.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.701
What made you want to add
a fourth hour to "Morning Joe"?

00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:50.267
-Well, as you can see,
three hours,

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:53.534
there's not enough time
in the day for him and talking.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:55.667
-Yeah.
-So, why not four?

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:56.934
-Yeah.
-Why not five?

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:58.934
-Why not five? You are
really good at it, though.

00:00:58.934 --> 00:01:00.167
-Well, that's very nice
of you to say.

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:01.767
-He's amazing.
-You're both great.

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:05.434
-Speaking of
good at something --

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:07.267
Questlove's not here.
-Oh, my God.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.567
-Man.
-No, but Questlove, yeah.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:09.701
We told him to stay in L.A....

00:01:09.701 --> 00:01:11.300
-"Summer of Soul"
is mind-blowing.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.434
-...'cause he just won a Grammy.
-It's unbelievable.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.400
-He won an Oscar last week,
and he won a Grammy last night.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.567
I'm like,
"Take the week, buddy."

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.067
-Amazing.
-Amazing.

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:22.033
-Is that not one of the most
mind-blowing --

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:23.601
It's not even
a music documentary.

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:27.267
It's music, it's pop culture,
it's race, it's everything.

00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:29.667
Politics.
-He did such a fantastic job.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.601
Did you have any idea
the show would be such a hit?

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:34.033
It's 15 years now.
-I did.

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.667
-I didn't, but she did.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.400
I was just trying to, like,
hold the job down.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.701
-10 seconds in.
10 seconds in, I looked at him,

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.467
and I said, "I need to talk
to you in the break."

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.968
And I brought them both --
Joe and Willie, to me,

00:01:45.968 --> 00:01:47.934
and I said,
"This is going to be amazing.

00:01:47.934 --> 00:01:50.634
This is going to be the buzziest
political talk show.

00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:52.601
You are going to be incredible.
-You believe that?

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:54.868
-You're going to be the voice
of politics on the landscape.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.667
And Willie, you're such a star,

00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:58.200
you're gonna have
your own network show."

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.567
-No way.
-She actually said that.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:03.501
And I swear to God, I was on
a week-to-week contract.

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:05.501
They kept trying to fire me.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:07.734
They'd like --
They'd get the bag over my head

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:09.000
and start escorting me
out the front door.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.267
-I mean, did they really?
-Yes.

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.501
-No, I swear to God.
They wanted to fire me so badly.

00:02:11.501 --> 00:02:13.400
-Hated him. Hated him.
-Hated my guts.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.467
And Mika --
-"Scarborough Country."

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.033
-Really?
-No passport required,

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:20.434
only common sense allowed, baby.
But anyway.

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.801
So, the show starts,
and five minutes in, she said,

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:25.367
"You know, this is gonna work."
-How about you?

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.801
Did you think you
were gonna be a hit?

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:30.667
-No. I am, like,
a month away from turning 55.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.100
I had no idea.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.267
I really thought
my days were numbered.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.534
-Really?
-Yes.

00:02:35.534 --> 00:02:37.067
-Look at what you've been doing.

00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:39.400
Every single morning, you guys
are trending on Twitter.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.634
Like, the top-five
trending tweets.

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:41.801
-Yeah, it's not a good thing.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:42.968
-That's never a good thing,
though.

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:44.067
-Oh, it is a good thing.
-Is that a good thing?

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.534
-When I see that,
I get very nervous.

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:47.901
-Oh, no, it's positive.
As far as Twitter goes.

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.968
-Sometimes some tweets
can be a little nasty.

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:51.267
-Yeah, maybe it's not.
I don't know.

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:53.767
But I know that it is,
according to the ones I see.

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:55.367
-Yeah.
-People -- they look to you.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:57.000
They want to have
the conversation.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.267
We need someone to look at
to tell us the news

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.267
and to talk about it,
and you guys are doing it.

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:02.868
And you're taking it
to another thing.

00:03:02.868 --> 00:03:04.767
You're doing --
What is "50 over 50"?

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:07.701
-Oh! That's my partnership
with "Forbes" and my platform,

00:03:07.701 --> 00:03:09.667
Know Your Value,
and it actually celebrates

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:13.200
women over 50 who are achieving
great impact in their careers.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.400
We had our first list last year,
and 10,000 submissions came in.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.100
We went global.
-Good for you.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.834
-We had an event in Abu Dhabi,
and now submissions are open

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:25.000
for the next U.S.
"50 over 50" list.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.834
So, women
can nominate themselves

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:28.400
right now at forbes.com.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.868
-That's fantastic
that you're doing that.

00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:31.634
I think it's awesome.
[ Applause ]

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:33.934
There's no --

00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:35.934
There's no shortage of news
for you guys to cover

00:03:35.934 --> 00:03:38.400
on "Morning Joe."
I think, right now, Ukraine,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.200
I guess, is the story
on everyone's minds.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.567
How are you dealing with that,

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:45.534
and how do you stay optimistic,
I guess?

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:50.467
-Well, you know,
the news can be very grim.

00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.400
There are positive things
to look at, though.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.934
You look at what
the Ukrainians have done,

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.033
it's nothing short
of extraordinary.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:03:59.300
I can't think --
You know, we grew up --

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.734
You know,
I grew up reading history.

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:02.901
-Yeah.
-Grew up, you know,

00:04:02.901 --> 00:04:06.567
reading about Winston Churchill,
about Britain,

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:09.601
like, standing alone
against Nazism.

00:04:09.601 --> 00:04:12.100
And you sit there,
and you see this guy --

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.234
Like, I don't know if you've
seen any of him on Netflix,

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:17.000
but this guy who was an actor
on a Ukrainian show.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.300
-He's funny.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.934
-He played a president.
-Played a president.

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:22.601
-It's amazing.
-And nobody would have ever

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:26.634
expected that this guy
would actually be

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:29.100
the defender
of democracy in Europe.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.367
We've not seen anything
like this.

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:33.367
And the --
You know, we always see --

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:35.367
I don't know about you,
but my boys and I will --

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:39.067
you know, we love "Star Wars,"
and we love "Lord of the --"

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:40.467
and we watch
all of these things.

00:04:40.467 --> 00:04:43.267
You're sitting there going,
"Yeah, you know, it never really

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.834
works that way in real life
where the underdog can --"

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:48.200
-A movie.
-There's no --

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.067
You look at this,
and you're like,

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:54.067
they're the resistance.
Of course, it's real life

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:58.734
and real horrors, but the fact
that the Ukrainians,

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:02.367
who Putin doesn't even see
as a country or a people...

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:05.601
-A blip on the ra-- yeah.
-...they have just decimated

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:09.834
outside of Kyiv, decimated,
like, one of the most feared

00:05:09.834 --> 00:05:12.133
armies on the planet.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:15.033
Who would have believed,
six months ago,

00:05:15.033 --> 00:05:18.234
that we would look to Ukraine
and go, "My God,

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:21.801
these people have reminded us
what freedom's about."

00:05:21.801 --> 00:05:24.300
It's not all the stupid things

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.567
that we've been fighting about
in America.

00:05:26.567 --> 00:05:29.067
You know, "I didn't get
a pickle on my hamburger.

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:32.133
You're infringing
on my freedoms."

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.000
"I have to wear a mask."
Like, whatever.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.434
Your opin-- No.
We see it day in and day out.

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.367
The Ukrainians are fighting
for their freedom.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:41.701
They're fighting
for their lives.

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:45.734
-In a divided country,
this is what we're uniting over,

00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:48.667
which I think is kind of
beautiful to see.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.567
-Well, you're seeing Republicans
and Dem --

00:05:50.567 --> 00:05:53.767
I talked to somebody really high
up at the White House last week.

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:56.801
They said "Republicans,
Democrats came together.

00:05:56.801 --> 00:05:59.534
We sat, we had a conversation
for 30 minutes,

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:02.300
and it was like humans
talking to each other.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.133
It was amazing!"
-"Is that possible?

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:05.501
Yeah.
We can do it, guys.

00:06:05.501 --> 00:06:07.400
We can talk to each other.
It's very possible."

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.601
-Yes.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.334
-Thank you to you guys.
Thank you for coming on.

00:06:10.334 --> 00:06:11.934
Thanks for everything you do
every single morning.

00:06:11.934 --> 00:06:14.334
Joe Scarborough,
Mika Brzezinski.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:19.300
Watch "Morning Joe" weekdays
on MSNBC, fourth hour.

